      "studies": [                                                                                                      
          {"study_index": 1, "title": "Unpublished rating scales ‐ a major source of bias in randomised controlled      
  trials of treatments for schizophrenia", "abstract": "BackgroundA recent review suggested an association between      
  using unpublished scales in clinical trials and finding significant results.AimsTo determine whether such an          
  association existed in schizophrenia trials.MethodThree hundred trials were randomly selected from the Cochrane       
  Schizophrenia Group's Register. All comparisons between treatment groups and control groups using rating scales were  
  identified. The publication status of each scale was determined and claims of a significant treatment effect were     
  recorded.ResultsTrials were more likely to report that a treatment was superior to control when an unpublished scale  
  was used to make the comparison (relative risk 1.37 (95% C11.12–1.68)). This effect increased when a 'gold-standard'  
