│     "title": "The clinical research for the treatment of schizophrenia",                                             │
│     "abstract": "Mental illness fails to capture public interest unless it strikes individuals or their family       │
│ members. No other health problem is as shrouded by public indifference and ignorance. And of all mental illnesses,   │
│ none suffers as much from indifference and ignorance as schizophrenia. For many years people afflicted with          │
│ schizophrenia, along with their families who support them, have borne the burden of this lack of public concern. It  │
│ has led to isolation and further heartache. Now a new threat has arisen that may eventually add a crippling injury   │
│ to this insult.For reasons that we find difficult to understand, the value of psychiatric treatments for all mental  │
│ disorders have been disparaged by the general public and by those responsible for reimbursement for the care of the  │
│ mentally ill. The critical determinants of value in health care involve the relationship of efficacy to cost in an   │
│ equation in which Value= Efficacy/Cost. Cost is a legitimate consideration for treatments of any condition, but too  │
│ frequently in discussion of treatments for the mentally ill, the question of cost is not even broached. Rather,      │
│ discussion stops because of the assumption that treatments are generally ineffective. Policy makers outside the      │
│ world of psychiatric treatment are poorly informed about the efficacy of our treatments and the strength of our data │
│ base to support that position. Webooks.google.com"                                                                   │
